PRIVATE REFLECTION: {
The consensus firmly establishes $n_1 = 6$ and $n_2 = 10$, as the minimal order of the Picard-Fuchs equation for the even-degree potential is $u_r(n) = 2\lfloor n/4 \rfloor$, satisfying $u_r(n) = n/2 - 1$ uniquely for $n \equiv 2 \pmod 4$. This yields the parameter $\alpha = (n_1 - 1)/n_2 = 1/2$. Evaluating the period function $T(1/2) = 2\pi \, {}_2F_1(1/6, 5/6; 1; 1/2)$ via Gauss's second summation theorem gives the closed form $\frac{3^{1/4}\Gamma(1/4)^2}{\sqrt{2\pi}} = \frac{2\pi^{3/2}}{\Gamma(7/12)\Gamma(11/12)}$. Colleague 2's alternative of $n_2 = 8$ is conclusively refuted by the strict positivity of the hypergeometric series and symplectic constraints on vanishing cycles.
}

